.TH QUADRF-ETHERNET 8 "August 2026" "QuadRF" "QuadRF"
.SH NAME
quadrf-ethernet \- DHCP client or direct-connect island on eth0
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quadrf-ethernet
.RI [ start | stop ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Waits briefly for carrier on eth0, then for a DHCP lease. If a global
address other than
.B 10.55.1.1
appears, the appliance stays a LAN client and publishes
.B quadrf.local
on that interface. No DHCP server is started, so a home router keeps
owning the subnet.
.PP
If nothing answers, eth0 becomes a direct-connect island at
.B 10.55.1.1/24
with dnsmasq handing
.B 10.55.1.2
through
.B 10.55.1.10
to the attached machine. There is no default route, so an unplugged
cable cannot take over outbound traffic.
.PP
.B quadrf-ethernet.service
runs this at boot. The ifupdown stanza in
.I /etc/network/interfaces.d/quadrf-eth0.cfg
runs it again when the cable is plugged in.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/dnsmasq.d/quadrf-eth0-direct.conf
DHCP range and names, only while direct-connect is up.
.TP
.I /etc/dnsmasq.d/quadrf-eth0-lan.conf
Names only, while eth0 holds a router lease.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR quadrf-hotspot (8),
.BR quadrf (1)
